{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# APPROVED BY THE OPERATOR — implement Option 3 (scheduled main-health CI, every 6 hours)\n\nThe research below is complete and the recommendation has been **approved**. Do not re-litigate\nthe approach; the operator's instruction and the constraints are recorded in\n\"Operator's decision\" immediately below, and the acceptance criteria at the end are binding.\n\n## Operator's decision (the thing to implement)\n\n1. **GitHub CI for `main` runs on a SCHEDULE, not on push to `main`.** Cadence: **every 6 hours**\n   — that is the signal density the operator wants. Use an **off-the-hour minute** (not `0`) so\n   this repo's runs do not pile onto the hour boundary with every other project on the platform.\n2. **Evaluate EVERY GitHub Actions workflow so the policy is CONSISTENT** — no workflow keeps a\n   push-on-`main` trigger merely because nobody looked at it. Each workflow gets an explicit,\n   recorded disposition.\n3. **External-integration jobs stay at MOST once a day.** Their frequency must not increase.\n   `external-integration.yml` is weekly today and stays weekly.\n4. **Gerrit-verify and the reconciler workflows MUST NOT be changed** — they are separate\n   mechanisms with their own semantics (`gerrit-verify.yaml`'s concurrency keys on\n   `GERRIT_CHANGE_ID` as a deliberate stale-patchset guard; the reconcilers deliberately run\n   with `cancel-in-progress: false`).\n5. **`workflow_dispatch` must remain (or be added)** on every main-health workflow, so anyone can\n   ask \"is `main` healthy?\" on demand without waiting for the tick.\n6. **Pay for the loss of per-commit attribution, explicitly.** Two items from the approved\n   recommendation are NOT optional:\n   - Write the **`git bisect run` command into the workflow's FAILURE OUTPUT**, so remediation is\n     one copy-paste. A scheduled run covers many commits; the first responder must not have to\n     reconstruct the technique under pressure.\n   - **Publish the last-known-green `main` SHA** so a bisect has a lower bound (Zuul's periodic\n     pipelines and vLLM's \"greenest commit on main\" practice are the precedent).\n\n## The confirmed mechanism (verify, do not re-derive)\n\n`test.yml` declares:\n\n```yaml\nconcurrency:\n  group: test-${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: true\n```\n\nOn `main`, `github.ref` is the constant `refs/heads/main`, so every push to `main` lands in ONE\nconcurrency group and cancels the previous run. Cancelled runs render as a red X on the commit\nlist, and GitHub offers no native way to suppress that (see mechanics below).\n\nNote that the affected workflows filter pushes with `branches-ignore: [\"feature/**\"]`, which\n**includes `main`** — so `main` must be added to that ignore list explicitly. Branch-push CI for\nNON-`main` branches is preserved.\n\n## The problem (original framing)\n\nGerrit is the source of truth; GitHub is a read-only mirror. Gerrit's `Verified` label (CI per\npatchset) is the real merge gate. GitHub Actions ALSO runs on `main` post-merge as a safety net.\n\nWhen commits land close together the mirror's `main` runs get CANCELLED as superseded, so\nGitHub shows a long trail of red X's on a `main` that is actually healthy.\n\nOperator's framing: do NOT spend cycles validating every individual commit — Gerrit's\nper-patchset gate is the protection. Main CI matters as a safety net, but the value is\n\"is main healthy NOW\", not \"was every commit green\". Mirror what comparable projects do.\n\n## What comparable projects actually do (VERIFIED via GitHub API)\n\n| Mirror repo | Workflows | Check runs on latest main commit |\n|---|---|---|\n| golang/go | NONE (.github/workflows 404) | **0** |\n| openstack/nova | NONE | **0** |\n| LibreOffice/core | lockdown.yml only (issue-locking bot) | **0** |\n| aosp-mirror/platform_frameworks_base | - | **0** |\n| qt/qtbase | ninja-build.yml, `on: push` | outlier - see below |\n\n**Four of five major Gerrit-primary projects surface NO per-commit CI on the GitHub mirror at\nall.** The mirror is a code mirror, full stop. Qt is the exception and its answer is to make the\njob cheap enough that cancellation never matters (\"finish as fast as possible\", system libs,\nccache) rather than to report per-commit truth.\n\nWhere their real post-merge signal lives:\n- **Go** - post-submit on LUCI (ci.chromium.org/p/golang), dashboard at build.golang.org. Never GitHub.\n- **OpenStack/Zuul** - the closest precedent to what the operator wants, and it is a FIRST-CLASS\n  FEATURE, not a workaround. Zuul's `post` pipeline uses the **supercedent** manager, which\n  collapses queued items per project-ref: \"If more than one additional item is enqueued for the\n  project-ref, previously enqueued items which have not started processing are removed.\" That is\n  literally \"test main's current state, not every commit\". Separate `periodic` (daily 02:00 UTC),\n  `periodic-weekly`, `periodic-stable` pipelines carry the health-over-time signal.\n- **Kubernetes Prow/Tide** - batches PRs, tests the batch, merges together. Tide's docs describe\n  NO post-merge branch testing; the model is entirely pre-merge.\n- **Chromium/ChromeOS** - post-submit builders trigger every **7-10 hours**, deliberately covering\n  many commits at once, with sheriffs watching. This is the direct answer to the bisect question.\n\n## GitHub Actions mechanics (VERIFIED against docs)\n\n- **Cancelled == red X cannot be fixed natively.** GitHub staff acknowledged it Dec 2021; still\n  open in 2026 (community discussion 8336).\n- **`cancel-in-progress: false` alone does NOT fix it.** With the default `queue: single`, an\n  existing PENDING run in the group is cancelled and replaced - you still get a red X, just on a\n  different run.\n- **A workflow cannot decline to report.** Actions creates CHECK RUNS intrinsically; the\n  `statuses:` permission governs only commit statuses pushed via API, so `statuses: none` does\n  nothing.\n- **The only \"hide\" path is deleting runs after the fact** (MercuryTechnologies/delete-cancelled-runs).\n- **GitHub merge queue is structurally inapplicable** - `merge_group` is driven by PRs entering a\n  queue; with Gerrit owning the merge decision there are no PRs and `main` advances by mirror push.\n\n## Options (option 3 was chosen)\n\n1. **Drop mirror CI entirely** (Go / OpenStack / LibreOffice / AOSP model). Zero red X's, zero\n   cycles. Cost: loses the safety net unless a Gerrit-side post-merge job replaces it.\n2. **Collapse instead of cancel** (Zuul supercedent semantics). GitHub cannot express this\n   natively; closest is today's behavior plus run deletion. Hack by its own author's admission.\n3. **Schedule-only main health run** (`on: schedule`, drop the `push` trigger for `main`).\n   The ONLY option that removes cancellations by construction rather than hiding them. Surfaces\n   as a workflow badge / Actions tab, not a per-commit mark - which matches the stated intent.\n   Cost: attribution.  **← CHOSEN, at a 6-hour cadence.**\n4. **Keep per-commit but make it cheap** (Qt model). Preserves attribution; costs cycles\n   proportional to commit rate.\n\n**The attribution cost, and how these projects pay it:** Chromium proves batching is survivable -\npost-submit every 7-10 hours, accepting a multi-commit regression range, closed by AUTOMATED\nbisection (Findit: median 14 min for compile failures, 24 min for swarmed gtest) plus a HUMAN on\nsheriff rotation plus tree-closure to stop the bleeding. OpenStack pays it differently: the gate\npipeline is strong enough that post-merge breakage is rare, and `post` jobs are largely artifact\npromotion rather than validation.\n\nBecause Gerrit's `Verified` gate already tests every patchset, a red scheduled run means one of:\na semantic conflict between two independently-verified changes, environmental/dependency drift,\nor a flake. The commit window between last-green and red is small enough that\n`git bisect run <the same CI script>` resolves it in log2(n) steps - typically 3-5 builds.\n\n## What NOT to do, and why\n\n- **delete-cancelled-runs**: makes the timeline LOOK right while destroying the record, and fails\n  open - a genuine failure racing a delete is indistinguishable.\n- **`cancel-in-progress: false` as a one-liner**: does not work (see mechanics above).\n- **GitHub merge queue**: structurally inapplicable.\n- **`queue: max`**: does produce all-green per-commit, but spends MORE cycles than today and\n  directly contradicts the stated intent.\n\n## Scope — the trigger inventory this change reasons over\n\nCandidates (run on push to `main` today):\n\n| Workflow | Trigger today | Cancels? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `test.yml` | push `branches-ignore: [feature/**]` + PR + dispatch | yes (`test-${{ github.ref }}`) |\n| `optionality.yml` | push `branches-ignore: [feature/**]` + PR + dispatch | no |\n| `verify-identity.yml` | push `branches-ignore: [feature/**]` + PR + dispatch | no |\n| `prompt-eval.yml` | push `branches-ignore: [feature/**]` + PR + dispatch + weekly | yes |\n| `codeql.yml` | push `branches: [main]` + weekly | yes |\n| `terraform-drift.yml` | push `branches: [main]` path-filtered `infra/terraform/**` + daily + dispatch | no |\n\nOut of scope, MUST NOT be modified: `gerrit-verify.yaml`, `reconcile-bridge.yml`,\n`reconcile-bridge-canary.yml`, `external-integration.yml`, `mirror-guard.yml`, `lockdown.yml`,\n`release.yml`, `jira-dc-*.yml`, `structured-output-baseline.yml`, and the four `workflow_call`\nreusables (`_artifact-probe.yml`, `_build-and-test.yml`, `_eval-discipline.yml`,\n`_optionality.yml`) — the reusables have no triggers of their own, so only their CALLERS change.\n\n## The safety property that makes this acceptable\n\nPre-merge gating is **unchanged**. The Gerrit `Verified` lane runs the reusable\n`_build-and-test.yml` (and `_artifact-probe.yml`) through `gerrit-verify.yaml`'s\n`workflow_dispatch`, which is triggered by Gerrit per patchset. Editing `test.yml`'s **triggers**\ncannot alter that lane, because the lane never reads `test.yml`. Every change still needs\n`LLM-Review +1` AND `Verified +1` to land. The mirror's `main` CI is, and remains, a safety net —\nthe change only alters WHEN the safety net runs, never what gates a merge.\n\n## Files this touches\n\n`.github/workflows/test.yml`, `.github/workflows/optionality.yml`,\n`.github/workflows/verify-identity.yml`, `.github/workflows/prompt-eval.yml`,\n`.github/workflows/codeql.yml`, `.github/workflows/terraform-drift.yml`, and the\nCI-parity/workflow-policy tests under `tests/`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. **No workflow runs on a push to `refs/heads/main`.** For every workflow in the candidate table,\n   a push whose ref is `refs/heads/main` matches NO trigger. Concretely: the four\n   `branches-ignore` workflows list `main` alongside `feature/**`; `codeql.yml` and\n   `terraform-drift.yml` drop their `push` key entirely (each had `branches: [main]` only).\n2. **Push CI for NON-`main` branches is preserved** where it exists today — the `branches-ignore`\n   workflows still run on a push to any branch other than `main` and `feature/**`.\n3. **`test.yml`, `optionality.yml`, and `verify-identity.yml` gain a 6-hourly schedule** at a\n   non-zero minute, giving four \"is main healthy\" runs per day.\n4. **`prompt-eval.yml` and `codeql.yml` KEEP their existing weekly schedules and are NOT moved to\n   6-hourly** — prompt-eval runs a live, billable LLM tier and codeql is a security scanner whose\n   cadence is deliberate; a 6-hourly cadence would be a 28x increase neither was asked for.\n5. **`terraform-drift.yml` keeps its existing daily sweep** as its main-health signal.\n6. **Every changed workflow retains `workflow_dispatch`** so on-demand \"is main healthy?\" works.\n7. **A failing scheduled/dispatched `main` run publishes a copy-pasteable remediation recipe** to\n   the run summary: the `git bisect start <bad> <last-green>` + `git bisect run` command using the\n   repo's own CI reproduction (`make check && make test`).\n8. **The last-known-green `main` SHA is published** in that same summary (resolved from the\n   workflow's own run history via the Actions API — no new state to persist, and the mirror is\n   read-only so nothing is pushed), and is emitted on success too so the value is discoverable\n   before an incident. If it cannot be resolved the report degrades gracefully and says so rather\n   than failing the run.\n9. **The external-integration tier does not run more often.** `external-integration.yml` is\n   untouched (weekly), and adding a schedule to `test.yml` must not make its live `external` job\n   fire — that job stays gated to an explicit human opt-in.\n10. **The forbidden set is byte-identical**: `git diff` shows no change to `gerrit-verify.yaml`,\n    `reconcile-bridge.yml`, `reconcile-bridge-canary.yml`, `external-integration.yml`,\n    `mirror-guard.yml`, or any `_*.yml` reusable.\n11. **Automated regression coverage exists** for the trigger policy — a test asserts, per\n    workflow, that a push to `refs/heads/main` does not match, that dispatch remains, and that the\n    forbidden set is unmodified — so the policy cannot silently regress.\n12. **Every touched workflow parses and lints**: `make lint` (which runs actionlint over all\n    workflows) is green, plus `make typecheck` and `make test`.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `make lint` (includes actionlint over every workflow), `make typecheck`, `make test`.\n- The new policy test under `tests/` covering AC 1, 2, 6, 9, 10.\n- Manual reasoning recorded in the change: for each changed workflow, the resulting `on:` block\n  and why `refs/heads/main` no longer matches.\n\n## Explicitly NOT determined (do not treat as settled)\n\n- Whether qt/qtbase sets a `concurrency` block - its all-green story is INFERRED from the trigger\n  and speed comments, not confirmed.\n- Whether OpenStack/Wikimedia `post` pipelines run VALIDATION or only artifact/doc publishing.\n- Whether AOSP/Chromium mirrors deliberately suppress checks or simply have no workflows - the\n  observable (0 check runs) was verified, the intent was not.\n- Whether Findit still runs today and whether it auto-reverts.\n- Kubernetes post-merge: verified only that Tide's docs do not cover it.\n- GA status of the `queue:` concurrency option on the free/public Actions tier.\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785941656972431001,"uuid":"630c8619-8dd3-4f06-a550-1fb6521ea6aa"}